p.src:
Along a right-moving light ray $x=t$, invariance of light speed requires $x'=t'$. Equation  then becomes $\begin{aligned}
x'&=(1-v)x f(v),\\
t'&=(1-v)x g(v),\end{aligned}$ and hence $f(v)=g(v).$ Thus one common scale factor remains: $\begin{aligned}
x'&=(x-vt)f(v),\\
t'&=(t-vx)f(v).
\end{aligned}$ This is essentially the economical line of argument used in Einstein’s first relativity paper: operational simultaneity and invariant light speed determine the transformation.

